
<h2>Accuracy in judging others' personality</h2>

<ol>
<li><i>How can we measure accuracy?</i>  <br>The correlation (=agreement)
between a social perceiver's rating and a target person's self-rating is a
common measure of accuracy.  Such correlations of personality ratings range
between 0 and .60.  To know how accurate we are with a given correlation
coefficient you can use the "binomial effect size display":  It describes your
accuracy in terms of percentages of being right when predicting a dichotomous
outcome (e.g., he will talk in front of the group vs. he will not).  Start with
the correlation coefficient (e.g., .40), divide it by 2, which gives the number
20.  Add this 20 to the guessing accuracy of 50%, making it 70%.  That is, with
a correlation of .40 you are right in 7 out of 10 cases (where random guessing
makes you right in 5 out of 10 cases).  <p>

<li><i>Does accuracy depend on the personality trait that we try to infer?</i>
<br>Yes; different personality dimensions allow different degrees of accuracy.
Using the "Big 5" taxonomy of personality traits, the following differences
have emerged across many studies (numbers are for rating strangers):<p>
<pre>
	Extraversion-introversion: r = .30-.40
	Conscientiousness	   r = .20-.40
	Openness to experience	   r = .20-.30
	Agreeableness		   r = .10
	Neuroticism		   r = .0
</pre>
It seems clear that more "visible" traits allow greater accuracy than less
visible traits<p>

<li><i>Does accuracy depend on the degree of acquaintanceship between
target and social perceiver?<br></i>Yes; ratings of friends reach
correlations of .50, with occasional .60 among long-term couples, and
somewhat lower values for neuroticism.<p>

<li><i>Does accuracy depend on the number of social perceivers?<br></i>Yes; if
we aggreagate (=average) the judgments of several social perceivers, some of
their errors and biases are averaged out and that aggregate judgment shows
greater accuracy than any one perceiver does.  For example, one friend's rating
may lead to an accuracy of .30; four friends' ratings averaged lead to an
accuracy of .50.  Caveat: The social perceivers may not discuss their ratings
before averaging, otherwise some individual errors and biases are transferred
to the group.  <p>
</ol>

<h2>Some factors that influence impressions of other people</h2>

<ol>
<li>Order (primacy effect in impression formation)<br>
<li>Negativity (cf. Skowronski & Carlston article)<br>
<li>Accessibility (cf. Higgins et al: "priming" of godd vs. bad traits
and subsequent judgments of ambiguous story of "Donald"; expectations
about teacher influenced by central traits cold vs. warm;
Snyder, Tanke, & Berscheid: self-fulfilling prophecy of phsyical
attractiveness has effect on target's actual behavior)
</ol>

<h2>Some factors that improve the accuracy of your impressions</h2>

<ol>
<li>Motivated accuracy (e.g., accountability or feedback on one's judgments)<br>
<li>Attention to sampling (both of situations and of people) <br>
<li>Alternative explanations (e.g,. Jeremiah was nasty; is he a nasty
person or might he have just had a loss or failure experience that put
him in a nasty mood?)<br>
<li>Awareness of own biases (schemata, expectations, favorite tratis)<br>
<li>Empirical research<br>
</ol>

<h2>Errors and biases in attribution</h2> 

Earl models of how people explain behavior ("attribute causes to
behavior") were more normative than descriptive (i.e., they laid out
ideal rules of how one might explain behavior instead of describing
how people actually explain behavior).  Because of this normative
orientation, research has focused on people's <i>deviations</i>
from these norms---the errors and biases one can find when people
explain behavior.  <ul> <li><b>Fundamental attribution error </b>(=
trait attr. &gt; sit.  attr.)<br>Jones &amp; Harris (1967): attitude
inference from Castro speech (crucial comparison of choice
vs. no-choice condition); Ross, Amabile &amp; Steinmetz (1977):
knowledge inferences for questioners and contestants in "quiz show"<p>

<li><b>Actor-observer asymmetry</b><br>Jones &amp; Nisbett (1972):
actors...situation causes; observers...person causes (traits); <b>Ex: </b>first
conversations; personality trait ratings ("depends on the situation"), choosing
a major<p>
</ul><p>

